/* CSO Cyber Range — Course content: BC-101 Intro to Cybersecurity & Resilience Frameworks */
window.COURSE_DATA = window.COURSE_DATA || {};
window.COURSE_DATA["BC-101"] = {
ov:"This course sets the vocabulary and mental models for everything that follows in the academy. You will move from the classic protection mindset — keep attackers out — to the resilience mindset that modern programs are built on: assume compromise, engineer systems that absorb and recover, and measure the program against mission outcomes rather than tool counts. The capstone draws two items from every module plus ten scenario items, so treat each knowledge check as a preview of the exam.",
mods:[
{t:"The CIA Triad and the Parkerian Hexad",d:"The security properties every control ultimately defends.",
obj:["Define confidentiality, integrity, and availability and give a concrete violation of each","Explain what the Parkerian Hexad adds and when the extra attributes matter","Map a given incident to the specific properties it violated"],
read:["Every security decision you will ever make traces back to three properties. Confidentiality means only authorized parties can read data — violated by a database dump posted online. Integrity means data is accurate and unaltered — violated when an attacker silently changes a payment routing number. Availability means systems are usable when needed — violated by ransomware that encrypts the file server. Together these form the CIA Triad, the oldest and most durable model in the field.",
"The triad is powerful but incomplete. Donn Parker extended it into the Parkerian Hexad by adding possession or control (a courier loses an encrypted backup tape — nothing was disclosed, yet you no longer control the media), authenticity (an email really came from who it claims), and utility (data is intact but useless, such as an encrypted file whose key was destroyed). These attributes capture real losses the triad cannot express, which matters when you write incident reports and risk statements.",
"In practice you will use these properties as an analytical checklist. When you evaluate a new system, ask what data it holds, then ask what a confidentiality loss, an integrity loss, and an availability loss would each do to the mission. The answers rarely carry equal weight: a public web brochure has almost no confidentiality requirement but a real availability one, while a targeting database inverts that. This asymmetry is the seed of impact categorization, which you will formalize in BC-105."],
ex:"A hospital scheduling system is hit by ransomware. Availability is violated (clinicians cannot see appointments), integrity is threatened (restored data may be stale), and possession is lost (attackers hold copies). Notice confidentiality may be intact if data was never exfiltrated — a precise property-by-property statement like this is exactly what leadership needs in the first report.",
refs:[["NIST SP 800-12 Rev.1, Ch.1-2","The introduction to information security concepts — read the sections defining the core security objectives."],["FIPS 199, Section 3","How the federal government formally defines confidentiality, integrity, and availability impact."]],
kc:[
{q:"An attacker silently alters the account number on stored invoices without anyone noticing. Which property is primarily violated?",o:["Confidentiality","Integrity","Availability","Utility"],a:1,x:"The data was changed from its accurate state — that is an integrity violation, even though nothing was disclosed or taken offline."},
{q:"A courier loses an encrypted backup tape. The encryption is strong and the key is safe. Which Parkerian attribute is lost?",o:["Confidentiality","Availability","Possession or control","Authenticity"],a:2,x:"No one can read the tape, so confidentiality holds — but the organization no longer controls the media. That is exactly the gap the hexad's possession attribute covers."},
{q:"Why is a property-by-property impact statement more useful to leadership than saying a system was hacked?",o:["It sounds more technical","It identifies which mission consequences actually occurred so response and reporting match reality","It reduces the number of controls needed","It removes the need for forensics"],a:1,x:"Precise property analysis tells decision-makers what was actually lost — disclosure, corruption, or downtime — which drives different responses."}]},

{t:"Threats, Vulnerabilities, and Risk",d:"The three-part equation behind every risk statement you will write.",
obj:["Distinguish threat, vulnerability, and risk with correct usage","Explain why risk requires both a threat and a vulnerability to exist","Write a basic risk statement in the condition-consequence format"],
read:["The field's most abused vocabulary is also its most important. A threat is any circumstance or actor with the potential to cause harm — a ransomware crew, a hurricane, a careless admin. A vulnerability is a weakness that could be exploited — an unpatched service, a missing process, an untrained user. Risk is the combination: the likelihood that a threat exercises a vulnerability, times the impact if it does. No threat, no risk. No vulnerability, no risk.",
"This is why a scanner finding is not automatically a risk. A critical CVE on a server that is powered off, isolated, and holds no data may represent near-zero risk; a medium CVE on an internet-facing authentication server may be the most dangerous thing in the enterprise. Context — exposure, data sensitivity, mission dependency — converts a vulnerability into a quantified risk. This distinction drives the prioritization discipline you will practice in IC-202.",
"Professionals communicate risk in a condition-consequence format: because of [vulnerability], a [threat] could [event], resulting in [mission impact], with likelihood [rating]. Writing risks this way forces you to state assumptions that others can challenge, and it gives the accepting authority a real decision to make. Vague statements like the system is vulnerable to attack are unfalsifiable and undecidable — the mark of an immature program."],
ex:"An analyst finds CVE-2024-3400 on a perimeter firewall. Threat: known active exploitation by multiple actors. Vulnerability: the unpatched PAN-OS service. Risk statement: because the management interface is internet-reachable and unpatched, an external actor could gain root on the perimeter device, exposing all tunneled traffic — likelihood HIGH given active exploitation, impact HIGH. That statement is decidable; a raw CVE number is not.",
refs:[["NIST SP 800-30 Rev.1, Ch.2","The federal risk assessment model — threat sources, threat events, vulnerabilities, likelihood, and impact."],["FAIR Institute, An Introduction to FAIR","A complementary, quantitative way to decompose risk into frequency and magnitude."]],
kc:[
{q:"A critical vulnerability exists on a server that is powered off in a locked vault with no network connection. What is the risk?",o:["Critical, because the CVSS score is critical","Near zero, because no plausible threat can currently exercise the vulnerability","High, because vaults can be broken into","Unknowable without a scanner"],a:1,x:"Risk requires a threat with a plausible path to exercise the vulnerability. Exposure context, not the raw score, determines risk."},
{q:"Which statement correctly uses the vocabulary?",o:["The hurricane is a vulnerability to our data center","The unpatched VPN is a threat","A ransomware crew is a threat; the unpatched VPN is a vulnerability; their combination creates risk","Risk and vulnerability are synonyms"],a:2,x:"Threat = potential source of harm, vulnerability = exploitable weakness, risk = likelihood times impact of the combination."},
{q:"What makes the condition-consequence risk format superior to saying the system is insecure?",o:["It is longer","It states assumptions and mission impact explicitly so an authority can actually decide to accept, mitigate, or transfer","It hides technical detail","It guarantees the risk is low"],a:1,x:"Decision-makers need a falsifiable statement with likelihood and impact — that is what turns analysis into an actionable decision."}]},

{t:"Threat Actors and Their Motivations",d:"Who is actually on the other side of the keyboard, and why it changes your defense.",
obj:["Differentiate the major threat actor classes by capability and intent","Explain why actor motivation changes which assets need the most defense","Identify the insider threat as both malicious and negligent"],
read:["Defenses are hypotheses about attackers, so you must know who they are. Nation-state actors (APTs) are well-resourced, patient, and mission-driven — they want intelligence, pre-positioning, or strategic effect, and they will spend months living off the land to avoid detection. Organized criminal groups are profit-driven: ransomware, business email compromise, and card theft dominate because they monetize quickly. Their tradecraft is increasingly rented — ransomware-as-a-service lowers the skill floor dramatically.",
"Hacktivists seek visibility and embarrassment — defacement, leaks, denial of service timed to news cycles. Script kiddies run tools they do not understand against everything; they are individually weak but numerically constant, which is why unpatched internet-facing services are found within hours. Insiders are the class defenders most underestimate: the malicious insider abuses legitimate access, while the negligent insider — far more common — clicks the phish, misconfigures the bucket, or emails the spreadsheet to the wrong domain.",
"Motivation tells you where to concentrate. Against criminals, make monetization hard: backups, segmentation, and rapid detection shrink the ransom business case. Against APTs, protect the crown jewels and hunt for persistence rather than trusting the perimeter. Against insiders, least privilege and behavioral monitoring matter more than firewalls. A program that cannot name its likely adversaries is defending everything equally, which means defending nothing well."],
ex:"A defense contractor and a regional retailer both find credential-stealing malware. For the retailer the likely actor is a criminal group heading toward payment data — priority: isolate the point-of-sale segment. For the contractor the same malware may be an APT foothold heading toward design documents — priority: hunt lateral movement toward engineering file shares. Same alert, different adversary hypothesis, different response.",
refs:[["Verizon DBIR, latest edition, Actors section","Annual empirical data on who actually causes breaches and why."],["MITRE ATT&CK, Groups pages","Documented behavior profiles of named intrusion sets — browse two or three to see how motivation shapes technique."]],
kc:[
{q:"Which actor class is typically characterized by patience, custom tooling, and intelligence-collection goals?",o:["Script kiddies","Nation-state APTs","Hacktivists","Negligent insiders"],a:1,x:"APTs are resourced for long campaigns and value stealth and persistence over quick monetization."},
{q:"Why does ransomware-as-a-service matter to defenders?",o:["It makes ransomware legal","It lowers the skill required to run sophisticated attacks, increasing attack volume","It only affects cloud systems","It eliminates phishing"],a:1,x:"When capability is rentable, many more actors operate at a high level — volume and reach increase even if elite skill does not."},
{q:"The most common insider threat manifestation is:",o:["A disgruntled admin planting a logic bomb","A negligent user mishandling data or falling for social engineering","A spy recruited by a foreign service","A contractor selling credentials"],a:1,x:"Malicious insiders exist, but negligent behavior — misdirected email, misconfiguration, phishing clicks — accounts for far more incidents."}]},

{t:"Security Control Types and Functions",d:"Preventive, detective, corrective, deterrent — and the administrative/technical/physical axes.",
obj:["Classify any control by function: preventive, detective, corrective, deterrent, compensating","Classify controls by implementation class: administrative, technical, physical","Explain why detection and correction deserve investment equal to prevention"],
read:["Controls are the atoms of a security program, and they classify along two axes. By function: preventive controls stop an event (firewall rule, disk encryption, MFA); detective controls identify that an event is occurring or occurred (IDS alerts, log review, file integrity monitoring); corrective controls limit damage and restore (backups, incident response, failover); deterrent controls discourage attempts (warning banners, visible cameras, prosecution policy); compensating controls substitute when a primary control is infeasible (network isolation standing in for patching on a legacy device).",
"By implementation class: administrative controls are policies, procedures, and training — they direct human behavior. Technical (logical) controls are enforced by systems — access control lists, cryptography, authentication. Physical controls protect the environment — locks, mantraps, guards, environmental sensors. Auditors expect you to name both axes: MFA is a technical preventive control; the incident response plan is an administrative corrective control.",
"Immature programs overspend on prevention because stopping attacks feels like the whole job. The resilience view is that prevention will eventually fail, so mean time to detect and mean time to recover are equally load-bearing. A program with excellent firewalls, no log review, and untested backups is one successful phish away from a catastrophic outage. When you design the deliverable for this course, you will be graded on functional balance, not tool count."],
ex:"For a legacy lab instrument that cannot be patched (vendor constraint), the team applies compensating controls: it is moved to an isolated VLAN (technical preventive), traffic to it is alerted on (technical detective), a spare image is maintained (corrective), and an SOP restricts USB use (administrative preventive). No single control is strong; the assembly is defensible, and each piece is nameable by type — which is exactly how you justify it in an authorization package.",
refs:[["NIST SP 800-53 Rev.5, Ch.2","How the control catalog is organized and what a control actually is."],["CIS Critical Security Controls v8, Overview","A prioritized, functionally balanced control set — note how many controls are detective and corrective."]],
kc:[
{q:"A file integrity monitoring agent that alerts when system binaries change is which functional type?",o:["Preventive","Detective","Corrective","Deterrent"],a:1,x:"It does not stop the change — it identifies that a change happened so responders can act. Identification is the detective function."},
{q:"An incident response plan is best classified as:",o:["Technical preventive","Administrative corrective","Physical detective","Technical deterrent"],a:1,x:"It is a documented procedure (administrative class) whose function is limiting damage and restoring operations (corrective)."},
{q:"Why do resilient programs invest heavily in detective and corrective controls?",o:["Preventive controls are too expensive to buy","Because prevention eventually fails, and time-to-detect plus time-to-recover determine actual mission damage","Auditors ignore preventive controls","Detection replaces the need for prevention"],a:1,x:"Assume breach: once prevention fails, the only variables left are how fast you notice and how fast you recover."}]},

{t:"Defense in Depth Architecture",d:"Layered, diverse, overlapping controls — and the failure modes of each layer.",
obj:["Describe the canonical defense-in-depth layers from perimeter to data","Explain why layer diversity matters as much as layer count","Identify single points of failure in a layered design"],
read:["Defense in depth is the design principle that no single control failure should equal mission failure. The canonical layers run from outside in: perimeter (firewalls, DDoS absorption, mail and web gateways), network (segmentation, internal firewalls, NDR), host (hardening, EDR, patching, local firewalls), application (secure coding, WAF, input validation, authentication), and data (encryption at rest and in transit, DLP, backups, rights management). Around all of them sit the human and governance layers: training, policy, and monitoring.",
"Depth is not redundancy of the same thing. Three firewalls from the same vendor in a row share bugs, misconfigurations, and a management console — one credential theft defeats all three. Effective depth is diverse: a phish that beats the mail gateway still has to beat user training, then EDR on the endpoint, then egress filtering, then the segmentation between the user LAN and the data it wants. Each layer buys detection opportunities and response time; that time is the real product.",
"When you evaluate a layered design, hunt for the choke points that collapse the layers: shared credentials that work at every tier, a management plane reachable from user space, backup infrastructure joined to the same domain it protects. Ransomware crews specifically target these collapse points — domain admin credentials and online backups — because they convert a one-layer breach into a total one. Your capstone deliverable will be scored on whether the layers fail independently."],
ex:"In the 2013 Target breach, attackers entered through an HVAC vendor's credentials, crossed a flat network to point-of-sale systems, and exfiltrated card data. A segmentation layer between vendor access and payment systems, or egress monitoring on POS segments, would each have broken the chain. The lesson is not that Target lacked controls — it is that the layers did not fail independently.",
refs:[["NSA, Defense in Depth guidance","The original articulation of layered defense across people, technology, and operations."],["CISA, Layering Network Security Through Segmentation","Practical segmentation patterns that make layers fail independently."]],
kc:[
{q:"Why is deploying three firewalls from the same vendor in series weak defense in depth?",o:["Firewalls are obsolete","They share bugs, configuration errors, and management plane — a common-mode failure defeats all three","Three is too few","Latency makes it unusable"],a:1,x:"Depth requires diversity. Identical controls fail identically; a shared vulnerability or stolen console credential collapses every layer at once."},
{q:"What is the primary product that each additional defensive layer buys?",o:["Compliance points","Detection opportunities and response time before mission impact","Lower licensing costs","Elimination of insider threat"],a:1,x:"Each layer an attacker must defeat creates noise and delay — time defenders can convert into detection and containment."},
{q:"Which design choice most dangerously collapses defense-in-depth layers?",o:["Different EDR and firewall vendors","Backups on a domain-joined server reachable with the same admin credentials as production","A DMZ for public services","Egress filtering on server segments"],a:1,x:"Ransomware actors hunt exactly this: one credential set that reaches both production and its backups converts a breach into an unrecoverable event."}]},

{t:"From Protection to Cyber Resilience",d:"Assume breach: absorb, adapt, withstand, and recover.",
obj:["Define cyber resilience and contrast it with pure prevention","Explain the four resilience outcomes: anticipate, withstand, recover, adapt","Explain graceful degradation and why it is a design requirement"],
read:["Protection-era security asked one question: how do we keep them out? Resilience accepts that a determined adversary, a zero-day, or an insider will eventually get in, and asks the harder question: when something fails, how quickly and gracefully does the organization absorb the hit, keep the mission running, and recover? NIST SP 800-160 Volume 2 frames this as four outcomes — anticipate adversity, withstand it while sustaining critical functions, recover them fully, and adapt so the same hit does not land twice.",
"Resilience is an engineering property, not a slogan. It shows up as design decisions: critical functions identified and ranked so degradation is deliberate rather than accidental; systems that degrade gracefully — a scheduling system that falls back to read-only beats one that goes dark; recovery paths that are tested, offline, and independent of the failed environment; and architectural adaptability, so lessons from incidents become configuration changes within days.",
"This mindset reallocates budget. A resilience-driven program measures mean time to detect, mean time to recover, and the blast radius of a credential compromise — and invests in whatever moves those numbers. It still buys prevention, because prevention shrinks event frequency, but it refuses to buy the tenth preventive tool while backups remain untested. The rest of this academy — hunting, forensics, IR command, capability frameworks — is resilience practice in different uniforms."],
ex:"Maersk's 2017 NotPetya recovery is the canonical case: the malware destroyed thousands of servers in minutes — prevention had already lost. What saved the company was a single surviving domain controller in Ghana (an accidental offline copy) plus a massive, improvised recovery capability. A resilience-engineered version of that story has deliberate offline replicas and a rehearsed rebuild plan, turning ten chaotic days into two planned ones.",
refs:[["NIST SP 800-160 Vol.2 Rev.1, Ch.1-2","The engineering definition of cyber resiliency — goals, objectives, and techniques."],["MITRE, Cyber Resiliency Engineering Framework","How resilience techniques map to concrete architectural decisions."]],
kc:[
{q:"Cyber resilience is best described as the ability to:",o:["Prevent every possible attack","Anticipate, withstand, recover from, and adapt to adverse cyber events while sustaining the mission","Pass an annual compliance audit","Eliminate all vulnerabilities"],a:1,x:"Resilience assumes some events will succeed and engineers the organization to keep operating and bounce back — the 800-160v2 framing."},
{q:"A scheduling system that automatically falls back to read-only mode during a database compromise demonstrates:",o:["Graceful degradation","Defense evasion","Threat hunting","Risk transference"],a:0,x:"Degrading to a reduced-but-useful state instead of failing completely is graceful degradation — a core resilience design property."},
{q:"Which metric set best indicates a resilience-oriented program?",o:["Number of firewalls and licensed tools","Mean time to detect, mean time to recover, and credential blast radius","Count of policies published","Antivirus signature version"],a:1,x:"Resilience is measured by how fast you notice, how fast you restore, and how far a single failure spreads — not by inventory."}]},

{t:"Resilience Engineering Techniques",d:"Redundancy, segmentation, deception, diversity, and non-persistence in practice.",
obj:["Describe five concrete resilience techniques and the failure each one blunts","Match a given mission requirement to appropriate resilience techniques","Explain the cost trade-offs resilience techniques impose"],
read:["NIST 800-160v2 catalogs resilience techniques you will see throughout this program. Redundancy — duplicate capability (secondary sites, replicated data, spare instruments) — blunts availability loss, but only if the replicas are isolated enough not to share the primary's fate. Segmentation — dividing systems by trust and function — limits blast radius; it is the single highest-value technique against both ransomware and APT lateral movement. Diversity — heterogeneous vendors, platforms, or paths — prevents common-mode failure, at real operational cost.",
"Deception — honeypots, honey tokens, decoy credentials — turns the attacker's need to explore into your detection opportunity; a tripped canary token is one of the highest-signal alerts that exists. Non-persistence — regularly re-imaging endpoints, short-lived cloud workloads, ephemeral credentials — shrinks the window in which a foothold survives. Add privilege restriction, analytic monitoring, and substantiated integrity (verifying that software and data are what they claim), and you have a toolbox rather than a slogan.",
"Every technique taxes something: redundancy costs money, diversity costs operational simplicity, non-persistence costs administrator convenience, segmentation costs network flexibility. Engineering judgment means matching technique to the mission functions that rank highest — you cannot afford everything everywhere. The pattern to internalize: identify critical functions first, then choose the two or three techniques that most cheaply keep those functions alive under your named threat scenarios."],
ex:"A range-instrumentation network cannot be patched during live-fire weeks. The engineering answer is layered resilience: segmentation isolates it from the enterprise, a warm spare (redundancy) sits imaged and offline, canary tokens (deception) sit on the file shares, and instruments are re-imaged between exercises (non-persistence). Patching still happens in maintenance windows — the techniques cover the gap, and each one is justified against a named failure mode.",
refs:[["NIST SP 800-160 Vol.2 Rev.1, Appendix D","The technique catalog — read the definitions for redundancy, segmentation, diversity, deception, and non-persistence."],["MITRE Engage","A framework for planning deception and adversary engagement operations."]],
kc:[
{q:"Which resilience technique most directly limits how far ransomware spreads after initial compromise?",o:["Deception","Segmentation","Diversity","Redundancy"],a:1,x:"Segmentation constrains lateral movement, capping blast radius — the primary variable in ransomware damage."},
{q:"A canary token placed in a file share fires an alert. Why is this signal so valuable?",o:["It proves the perimeter works","Legitimate users have no reason to touch it, so a hit almost certainly indicates unauthorized exploration","It automatically removes the attacker","It replaces the SIEM"],a:1,x:"Deception assets have near-zero false-positive rates — nobody should touch them, so any touch is high-confidence signal."},
{q:"Re-imaging endpoints on a regular cycle is an example of which technique, and what does it buy?",o:["Diversity — it prevents vendor lock-in","Non-persistence — it shortens the lifetime of any foothold an attacker establishes","Redundancy — it duplicates data","Privilege restriction — it removes admin rights"],a:1,x:"Non-persistence resets systems to known-good state, evicting persistence mechanisms the defender never even detected."}]},

{t:"The NIST Cybersecurity Framework",d:"Govern, Identify, Protect, Detect, Respond, Recover — the common language of programs.",
obj:["Name the six CSF 2.0 functions and what each covers","Explain how profiles and tiers are used to plan improvement","Use the CSF to identify programmatic gaps"],
read:["The NIST Cybersecurity Framework is the most widely spoken common language for security programs. CSF 2.0 organizes outcomes into six functions. Govern establishes strategy, roles, policy, and supply chain expectations — elevated to a function because leadership decisions shape everything else. Identify covers knowing your assets, risks, and dependencies. Protect covers safeguards: identity, training, data security, platform hardening. Detect covers finding adverse events; Respond covers acting on them; Recover covers restoration and communication during rebuilding.",
"The framework's practical machinery is profiles and tiers. A current profile is an honest inventory of which outcomes you achieve today; a target profile is where the mission needs you to be; the delta between them is your improvement roadmap, prioritized and budgeted. Implementation tiers (Partial, Risk Informed, Repeatable, Adaptive) describe how mature and institutionalized the practice is — a Tier 1 program does things heroically and inconsistently; a Tier 4 program adapts based on measurement.",
"The CSF deliberately does not prescribe controls — it maps to catalogs like SP 800-53 and CIS Controls, which supply the how. Its value is diagnostic and communicative: a one-page current-versus-target profile is the most efficient artifact ever devised for explaining a security program to an executive. When you hear a program described as strong on Protect, weak on Detect and Recover, you are hearing a CSF diagnosis — and after this course you should immediately suspect what that imbalance means for resilience."],
ex:"A new CISO inherits a program with excellent firewalls and encryption but no asset inventory, no log aggregation, and untested backups. In CSF terms: Protect is mature, Identify, Detect, and Recover are near-absent. The one-page profile showing this imbalance — and a target profile with dated milestones — wins budget in a single board meeting, because the gap is visible without any technical vocabulary.",
refs:[["NIST CSF 2.0","Read the function and category descriptions plus the section on profiles and tiers."],["NIST CSF 2.0 Quick Start Guides","Short implementation guides — skim the one on creating organizational profiles."]],
kc:[
{q:"Which function did CSF 2.0 add to elevate strategy, roles, and supply chain oversight?",o:["Identify","Govern","Respond","Protect"],a:1,x:"Govern was promoted to a full function in CSF 2.0, recognizing that leadership and policy decisions underpin all other outcomes."},
{q:"In CSF practice, the gap between the current profile and the target profile is:",o:["A compliance violation","The prioritized improvement roadmap","A reason to change frameworks","The audit scope"],a:1,x:"Profiles are diagnostic: current state versus mission-required state, with the delta becoming the plan."},
{q:"A program described as strong on Protect but weak on Detect and Recover is most exposed to:",o:["Regulatory fines only","Long-dwell intrusions and slow, chaotic restoration after the inevitable successful attack","Excessive licensing costs","Nothing — Protect is sufficient"],a:1,x:"Without Detect, breaches dwell for months; without Recover, the first successful attack becomes a prolonged outage. Prevention alone is brittle."}]},

{t:"ISO/IEC 27001 and the ISMS",d:"Management-system thinking: risk-driven, auditable, continuously improved security.",
obj:["Explain what an ISMS is and what ISO 27001 certification attests","Describe the Plan-Do-Check-Act improvement cycle","Contrast ISO 27001 with NIST CSF and SP 800-53 in purpose and audience"],
read:["ISO/IEC 27001 certifies a management system, not a network. An Information Security Management System (ISMS) is the governance machinery — leadership commitment, risk assessment method, statement of applicability, internal audit, management review, corrective action — that ensures security decisions are made deliberately and improve over time. Certification by an accredited body attests that this machinery exists and operates; it does not attest that any particular technology is deployed.",
"The engine inside every ISMS is Plan-Do-Check-Act. Plan: assess risks and select controls (documented in the Statement of Applicability, drawing on the Annex A control set). Do: implement and operate them. Check: audit, measure, and review. Act: correct nonconformities and improve. This loop is why mature organizations treat audit findings as fuel rather than embarrassment — the system is designed to surface and consume its own defects.",
"Position the frameworks correctly: ISO 27001 is an international, certifiable management standard — common in commercial and multinational contexts where customers demand third-party attestation. NIST CSF is a free diagnostic and communication framework. NIST SP 800-53 is a detailed control catalog mandated in US federal systems through the RMF. They interoperate: an organization can use CSF language for strategy, 27001 for management-system certification, and 800-53 or Annex A as the control catalog underneath. Knowing which artifact a stakeholder actually needs is a professional skill."],
ex:"A cloud provider selling to both EU commercial customers and the US DoD runs one ISMS: ISO 27001 certification satisfies the commercial customers' due-diligence demands, while the same asset inventories, risk registers, and audit evidence feed its FedRAMP/RMF package for federal work. One management system, two attestation surfaces — the efficient pattern you should recommend when both markets matter.",
refs:[["ISO/IEC 27001:2022, Clauses 4-10","The management-system requirements — what certification actually audits."],["ISO/IEC 27002:2022, Introduction","How the Annex A controls are organized and applied."]],
kc:[
{q:"ISO 27001 certification primarily attests that an organization:",o:["Uses a specific firewall vendor","Operates a functioning, risk-driven security management system with audit and improvement loops","Has never been breached","Encrypts all data with AES-256"],a:1,x:"The certificate covers the management system — governance, risk process, audit, improvement — not specific technologies or outcomes."},
{q:"In the PDCA cycle, an internal audit that finds a control operating ineffectively belongs to which phase, and what should follow?",o:["Plan — rewrite the policy","Check — followed by corrective action in the Act phase","Do — ignore it","Act — hide the finding before certification"],a:1,x:"Audits are the Check phase; the system is designed so findings drive Act-phase corrections. Surfacing defects is the mechanism, not a failure."},
{q:"A US federal system owner asks which framework is mandatory for their authorization. The correct answer is:",o:["ISO 27001, because it is international","NIST RMF with SP 800-53 controls — ISO and CSF may inform but do not replace it","CIS Controls only","Whichever is cheapest"],a:1,x:"Federal systems are authorized under the RMF using the 800-53 catalog; ISO 27001 and CSF are complementary, not substitutes, in that context."}]},

{t:"The DoD Risk Management Framework in Context",d:"Authorization, ongoing assessment, and where you fit in the machine.",
obj:["Summarize the seven RMF steps and the purpose of authorization","Identify the key RMF roles: AO, ISSM, ISSO, SCA","Explain continuous monitoring as the step that never ends"],
read:["Federal and DoD systems operate under the Risk Management Framework of NIST SP 800-37: a seven-step lifecycle — Prepare, Categorize, Select, Implement, Assess, Authorize, Monitor — that converts security work into a formal, accountable risk decision. The output of the first six steps is an authorization package (system security plan, assessment report, plan of action and milestones) on which a senior official, the Authorizing Official, personally accepts residual risk by granting an Authorization to Operate.",
"Learn the cast now, because you will work with all of them. The AO owns the risk decision. The Information System Security Manager (ISSM) runs the security program for a system or portfolio; the Information System Security Officer (ISSO) executes daily — control implementation evidence, POA&M hygiene, continuous monitoring artifacts. The Security Control Assessor (SCA) independently tests controls and writes the assessment report the AO relies on. Independence between implementer and assessor is what gives the package integrity.",
"The step outsiders miss is Monitor: authorization is not a diploma but a lease. Controls are reassessed on a cadence, changes are analyzed for security impact, and the POA&M tracks every known weakness to closure. A package that was accurate at signing and never updated is a liability — inspectors compare documents to the as-built system and every delta undermines the AO's decision. BC-105 walks the early steps in detail; IC-204 drills the eMASS mechanics of steps four through six."],
ex:"An ISSO inherits a system whose ATO was signed two years ago. Since then, a new interconnection was added and never assessed. In RMF terms this is an unanalyzed change operating outside the authorization boundary decision — the ISSO's move is a security impact analysis, an updated SSP, and either a minor-change memo or a significant-change package to the AO. Doing nothing quietly converts an engineering change into a false statement to the authorizing official.",
refs:[["NIST SP 800-37 Rev.2, Ch.2-3","The RMF steps, roles, and authorization decisions — the canonical source."],["DoDI 8510.01","How the DoD implements the RMF, including reciprocity and eMASS."]],
kc:[
{q:"An Authorization to Operate represents:",o:["A permanent certification the system is secure","A senior official's formal, personal acceptance of the system's residual risk, subject to continuous monitoring","Proof that no vulnerabilities exist","A vendor warranty"],a:1,x:"The ATO is a risk-acceptance decision by the AO based on the package — conditional, revocable, and dependent on the monitoring that follows."},
{q:"Why must the Security Control Assessor be independent of the implementation team?",o:["To slow the project down","So the assessment the AO relies on is unbiased by the implementers' interests","Because implementers lack clearances","Independence is optional"],a:1,x:"The AO's decision is only as good as the assessment evidence; independence prevents grading your own homework."},
{q:"A new external interconnection is added after ATO without analysis. The correct characterization is:",o:["Routine maintenance requiring nothing","An unanalyzed change to the authorization basis requiring security impact analysis and AO notification","A problem only at reauthorization time","The SCA's fault"],a:1,x:"The ATO covers a described system; material undescribed changes silently invalidate the risk decision until analyzed and reported."}]},

{t:"Security Governance, Policy, and Roles",d:"Policies, standards, procedures, guidelines — and who is accountable for what.",
obj:["Distinguish policy, standard, procedure, and guideline with examples","Explain accountability versus responsibility in security roles","Describe why governance failures defeat technical excellence"],
read:["Governance is how an organization decides, directs, and verifies its security posture — and its documents form a strict hierarchy. A policy states leadership intent and requirement at a high level (all remote access requires multi-factor authentication). A standard makes it specific and mandatory (remote access uses the enterprise VPN with FIPS-validated cryptography and phishing-resistant MFA). A procedure gives step-by-step instructions to achieve it (the ten steps to enroll a token). A guideline is recommended practice that leaves room for judgment. Confusing these produces unenforceable policy and unauditable practice.",
"Roles carry different weights. Accountability is singular and non-delegable — the AO is accountable for the risk decision, the CISO for the program. Responsibility is distributed and delegable — the ISSO is responsible for evidence collection, admins for hardening. The classic governance failure is diffuse accountability: when everyone owns security, no one does. RACI-style clarity (who is Responsible, Accountable, Consulted, Informed) for each major security process is a cheap, high-yield governance artifact.",
"Technical excellence cannot survive governance failure. Unowned policies drift out of date; controls without an accountable owner silently decay; exceptions granted verbally become permanent invisible risk. Conversely, strong governance makes ordinary technology effective, because every control has an owner, a review date, and an enforcement path. When later courses discuss policy engines and capability audits, they are automating exactly this discipline."],
ex:"An audit finds that a firewall rule permitting a vendor's access was added under a verbal exception three years ago; the vendor contract ended two years ago. No document, no owner, no expiry. The technical fix takes five minutes; the governance fix is an exception process with a named approver, a mandatory expiration date, and quarterly review of all active exceptions — preventing the entire class of finding.",
refs:[["NIST SP 800-100, Ch.2-3","Information security governance and the policy hierarchy for managers."],["SANS Security Policy Templates","Concrete examples showing the policy/standard/procedure distinction in practice."]],
kc:[
{q:"All remote access must use MFA is a policy. The document specifying the approved VPN product and token types is a:",o:["Guideline","Standard","Procedure","Baseline waiver"],a:1,x:"Standards translate policy intent into specific mandatory requirements; procedures then give the step-by-step how."},
{q:"Accountability differs from responsibility in that accountability:",o:["Can be delegated to contractors","Rests with one named individual and cannot be delegated, even when tasks are","Applies only to technical staff","Is another word for blame"],a:1,x:"Tasks distribute; ownership of the outcome does not. The AO can delegate work but not the risk decision."},
{q:"A verbal, never-expiring firewall exception for a departed vendor is best prevented by:",o:["A better firewall","A documented exception process with named approvers, expiry dates, and periodic review","Blocking all vendors","Annual penetration tests"],a:1,x:"The failure is governance, not technology — undocumented exceptions accumulate as permanent invisible risk until a process forces review."}]},

{t:"Measuring Security: Metrics and Maturity",d:"From vanity counts to decision-driving measurement.",
obj:["Distinguish vanity metrics from decision-useful metrics","Define MTTD, MTTR, patch latency, and coverage metrics","Explain maturity models and their honest use"],
read:["What you measure is what you manage, and most security programs measure the wrong things. Vanity metrics — blocked attacks, alerts generated, tools deployed — grow when you buy more sensors and say nothing about outcomes. Decision-useful metrics change behavior: mean time to detect (how long intrusions dwell), mean time to respond and recover, patch latency by severity band (days from patch release to deployment on exposed assets), control coverage (what fraction of endpoints actually run EDR), and phishing-report rate versus click rate.",
"Good metrics share three properties: they are tied to a decision (if patch latency exceeds SLA, we add maintenance windows), they are trended rather than snapshotted (a single number has no meaning; a slope does), and they resist gaming (if analysts are graded on tickets closed, tickets will close — quality unmeasured). Pair every rate metric with a quality check: MTTR that drops because responders skip forensics is not improvement.",
"Maturity models (CMMI-style levels, or CSF tiers) describe how institutionalized a practice is: ad hoc, repeatable, defined, measured, optimizing. Their honest use is self-diagnosis and roadmap sequencing — get logging repeatable before buying analytics; get inventory defined before automating response. Their dishonest use is trophy hunting, where the assessment is negotiated rather than evidenced. An auditor-proof maturity claim always points at artifacts: this process is level three because here is the procedure, the training record, and the last four executions."],
ex:"A SOC reports 40,000 blocked attacks monthly, and leadership learns nothing. Reframed: median dwell time fell from 21 days to 6 after EDR deployment; critical patch latency on internet-facing assets is 9 days against a 14-day SLA; EDR coverage is 91% with the gap enumerated. Same program, but now every number either confirms a control is working or names the next investment — that is measurement doing its job.",
refs:[["NIST SP 800-55 Rev.2","Measurement guide for information security — selecting and using metrics that support decisions."],["Verizon DBIR, Timeline sections","Industry dwell-time and discovery data — the external benchmark for your MTTD."]],
kc:[
{q:"Number of attacks blocked this month is a weak metric primarily because:",o:["It is hard to count","It scales with sensor count and attacker volume, not with defensive effectiveness, and drives no decision","Executives dislike large numbers","Firewalls cannot count accurately"],a:1,x:"It rises when you deploy more sensors or attackers scan more — it cannot distinguish a strong program from a noisy one."},
{q:"Mean time to detect measures:",o:["Scanner speed","How long adversary activity persists before the defender identifies it","Patch deployment speed","Alert queue length"],a:1,x:"MTTD is dwell time — the window in which an intruder operates unnoticed. Shrinking it is a primary resilience objective."},
{q:"The honest use of a maturity model is:",o:["Negotiating the highest defensible level for marketing","Self-diagnosis with artifacts as evidence, sequencing improvements from ad hoc toward measured","Replacing risk assessment","Ranking employees"],a:1,x:"Maturity models sequence roadmaps and expose institutionalization gaps — claims must trace to artifacts, not aspirations."}]},

{t:"Business Continuity and Disaster Recovery Basics",d:"RTO, RPO, BIA — keeping the mission alive when systems die.",
obj:["Define RTO and RPO and derive them from mission needs","Explain the business impact analysis and its outputs","Describe backup strategies including the 3-2-1 rule and immutability"],
read:["Continuity planning answers two numbers for every business function. Recovery Time Objective (RTO): how long the function can be down before damage is unacceptable. Recovery Point Objective (RPO): how much data, measured in time, you can afford to lose — the gap between the last good copy and the failure. A payroll run may tolerate an RTO of days; a command-and-control feed may tolerate seconds. These numbers are mission decisions extracted from process owners, not IT preferences — and every technology choice (backup frequency, replication, failover) is just a means of hitting them.",
"The Business Impact Analysis is the systematic version of that extraction: enumerate functions, map their dependencies (systems, people, facilities, vendors), and quantify impact over time to derive RTO and RPO per function. The BIA's ranked output tells you which ten systems deserve hot failover and which two hundred can wait for rebuild — without it, recovery spending is guesswork and the loudest voice wins.",
"Backup strategy earns special attention because ransomware weaponized it. The 3-2-1 rule — three copies, two media types, one offsite — now requires a modern amendment: at least one copy immutable or offline, unreachable by the credentials that run production, because attackers deliberately encrypt or delete online backups first. And an untested backup is a hope, not a control: restoration exercises, timed against RTO, are the only proof. Disaster recovery is the IT subset of continuity; the full discipline includes people, workarounds, and communication when systems stay down anyway.",],
ex:"A logistics unit sets RTO 4 hours and RPO 15 minutes for its dispatch system after the BIA shows trucks idle within the hour of an outage. Engineering translates: database replication every 5 minutes to a second site (meets RPO), automated failover rehearsed quarterly and last timed at 90 minutes (meets RTO), plus weekly immutable snapshots held 30 days in a separate account against ransomware. Every element traces to a mission number — that is the audit-proof pattern.",
refs:[["NIST SP 800-34 Rev.1, Ch.2-3","Contingency planning — BIA method, RTO/RPO derivation, and plan structure."],["CISA, Ransomware Guide (backup guidance)","Why offline/immutable copies are now mandatory practice and how to structure them."]],
kc:[
{q:"RPO answers which question?",o:["How long can we be down?","How much data, measured in time since the last good copy, can we afford to lose?","How many backups exist?","How fast is the network?"],a:1,x:"RPO bounds acceptable data loss and directly sets backup or replication frequency; RTO bounds acceptable downtime."},
{q:"The modern amendment to the 3-2-1 backup rule driven by ransomware is:",o:["All copies in the same cloud account for speed","At least one copy immutable or offline, unreachable with production credentials","Two copies are enough with encryption","Backups only need testing after an incident"],a:1,x:"Attackers hunt online backups with stolen admin credentials before detonating — an immutable or offline copy is the recovery guarantee."},
{q:"Who properly sets the RTO for a business function?",o:["The backup administrator based on tool capability","The mission/process owner based on impact over time, with IT engineering to meet it","The vendor","The auditor"],a:1,x:"RTO/RPO are mission-impact decisions surfaced by the BIA; technology is selected to achieve them, not the reverse."}]},

{t:"Security Culture and the Human Layer",d:"Training that changes behavior, reporting without fear, and usable security.",
obj:["Explain why usability failures become security failures","Describe the properties of effective awareness programs","Explain why no-blame reporting accelerates detection"],
read:["Humans are not the weakest link; they are the layer you refused to engineer. When security is unusable — password rules that force rotation into predictable patterns, VPNs that drop constantly, approval flows that take days — people build workarounds, and workarounds are invisible vulnerabilities: the shared spreadsheet of credentials, the personal Dropbox, shadow IT. Usable security is therefore a control property: the secure path must be the easy path, or it will not be the taken path.",
"Awareness programs change behavior only when they are specific, frequent, and relevant. Annual generic slideshows measurably do not work. What does: short role-targeted content (finance staff drill BEC and payment-change fraud; engineers drill credential and repo hygiene), realistic phishing exercises used as teaching moments rather than punishments, and metrics that track behavior change — report rates rising, repeat-click rates falling — instead of completion percentages.",
"The highest-yield cultural asset is a no-blame reporting norm. The user who clicks a phish and reports it within minutes has converted a potential three-week dwell into a same-hour containment; the user who fears punishment stays silent, and silence is what long dwell times are made of. Programs that celebrate fast reporting — publicly, sincerely — buy themselves a distributed sensor network of every employee. Combined with the metrics module: phishing report rate is a better health indicator than click rate, and the ratio between them is the culture metric."],
ex:"After a spearphish compromise, one command punished the clicker; a sister unit ran a blameless review and publicized that a different employee's fast report had triggered containment in 40 minutes. A year later the punishing command's report rate had collapsed to near zero — users deleted suspicious mail silently — while the blameless unit's median report time was 11 minutes. Same tooling, opposite outcomes: culture was the control.",
refs:[["NIST SP 800-50 Rev.1","Building a security awareness and training program that targets behavior, not attendance."],["SANS Security Awareness Report, latest edition","Empirical data on what makes awareness programs mature and effective."]],
kc:[
{q:"Unusable security controls tend to produce:",o:["Higher compliance","Invisible workarounds that become unmanaged vulnerabilities","Better audit results","Fewer help-desk tickets"],a:1,x:"When the secure path is painful, users route around it — and the workaround is outside every control and every log."},
{q:"The strongest indicator of a healthy human-layer program is:",o:["100% annual training completion","A high and fast phishing report rate relative to click rate","Zero clicks ever","Strict punishment of clickers"],a:1,x:"Reports are the behavior that shortens dwell time; completion percentages measure attendance, not behavior."},
{q:"Why does punishing users who report their own phishing clicks backfire?",o:["It violates most laws","It suppresses reporting, so future compromises dwell silently instead of being contained in minutes","It has no effect","Users forget the training"],a:1,x:"Fear converts your fastest sensors into silent ones; the click still happens, but the report — the thing that saves you — stops."}]},

{t:"Case Study Capstone: Designing a Defense-in-Depth Program",d:"Apply the entire course to one organization, end to end.",
obj:["Synthesize properties, actors, controls, and resilience techniques into one coherent design","Justify each design decision against a named threat and mission function","Recognize the analytical sequence: mission, threats, functions, controls, measurement"],
read:["This module rehearses the analytical sequence you will reuse for the rest of your career. Scenario: Meridian Logistics, 800 staff, runs freight dispatch for regional military exercises. Crown jewels: the dispatch database (availability-critical, RTO 4h), customer contract data (confidentiality-critical), and GPS telemetry integrity. Likely actors from module three's lens: criminal ransomware crews (opportunistic, monetization-driven) and one capable regional adversary interested in exercise logistics (patient, intelligence-driven). That pair of hypotheses drives everything that follows.",
"Design walk: start at the properties (module one) — rank C, I, and A per asset; dispatch is A-first, contracts are C-first, telemetry is I-first. Choose controls by function (module four) ensuring detective and corrective depth, layer them (module five) so failures are independent, then add resilience techniques (module seven): segmentation between corporate and dispatch, immutable backups sized to the 4-hour RTO (module thirteen), canary tokens in the contract share, and re-imaged dispatch terminals. Governance (module eleven) assigns every control an owner and expiry-dated exceptions; metrics (module twelve) track MTTD, patch latency, and report rate; the human layer (module fourteen) drills dispatchers on BEC because payment fraud is the criminal actor's cheapest path.",
"Notice what the sequence prevents. Starting from tools produces shelfware and unbalanced functions; starting from compliance produces paper mirroring; starting from mission functions and named adversaries produces a design where every control has a reason someone can say out loud. In the graded deliverable you will produce exactly this: a one-page layered architecture plus a justification table — control, layer, function type, threat addressed, mission function protected, owner, metric. That table is the whole course in one artifact."],
ex:"A draft Meridian design placed backups on a domain-joined NAS for convenience. Red-team review (module five thinking) flagged the collapse point: the ransomware actor's stolen domain-admin credential reaches both production and recovery. The corrected design moved weekly fulls to an isolated account with immutability — a one-line change on paper that converts a program-ending scenario into a 4-hour restoration, and the single most instructive fix in the whole exercise.",
refs:[["CISA, Cross-Sector Cybersecurity Performance Goals","A prioritized floor of practices — check the Meridian design covers every applicable goal."],["NIST SP 800-160 Vol.2 Rev.1, Appendix D","Revisit the technique catalog while placing resilience techniques in the design."]],
kc:[
{q:"The correct starting point for the Meridian design is:",o:["Selecting an EDR vendor","Ranking mission functions and security properties per asset, then hypothesizing likely adversaries","Writing the acceptable use policy","Buying cyber insurance"],a:1,x:"Mission functions plus named threat hypotheses generate requirements; tools and policies are chosen to meet them, never the reverse."},
{q:"The domain-joined backup NAS was flagged because:",o:["NAS devices are always slow","One stolen credential set reached both production and recovery, collapsing independent layers into a single failure","It violated the color scheme of the diagram","Backups are unnecessary with EDR"],a:1,x:"Layer independence is the point of defense in depth — recovery infrastructure must not share the failure domain of what it protects."},
{q:"The justification table (control, function, threat, mission function, owner, metric) primarily guards against:",o:["Budget overruns only","Controls that exist without a nameable reason — unowned, unmeasured shelfware","Long meetings","Vendor lock-in"],a:1,x:"If a control cannot be traced to a threat, a mission function, an owner, and a measure, it is cost without accountable value."}]}
],
cap:[
{q:"SCENARIO: A wiper destroys 300 servers at a shipping firm in 20 minutes. Post-incident review should conclude that further preventive spending is:",o:["The only priority","Insufficient alone — investment must shift toward tested recovery, offline replicas, and segmentation that caps blast radius","Wasteful and should stop entirely","A legal requirement"],a:1,x:"Once an event can succeed at machine speed, resilience outcomes — withstand and recover — determine mission damage, not marginal prevention."},
{q:"SCENARIO: Leadership asks why the program needs money for detection when the firewall already blocks millions of attacks monthly. The best response uses:",o:["The blocked-attack count as proof of success","Dwell-time framing: prevention eventually fails, and undetected intrusions currently persist for weeks — MTTD is the number that bounds damage","A threat actor documentary","A bigger firewall quote"],a:1,x:"Blocked-attack counts are vanity metrics; dwell time connects detection investment directly to mission damage."},
{q:"SCENARIO: An encrypted laptop with the only copy of a negotiation file is stolen, and the decryption key is safe with the owner. Using the Parkerian lens, what was lost?",o:["Confidentiality and integrity","Possession and availability — no disclosure occurred, but the organization lost control of media and access to the data","Nothing, because encryption held","Authenticity only"],a:1,x:"Encryption preserved confidentiality, but the sole copy is gone (availability) and the media is uncontrolled (possession) — the hexad expresses what the triad blurs."},
{q:"SCENARIO: A regional utility must choose where segmentation delivers the most resilience per dollar. The strongest placement is:",o:["Between marketing and HR file shares","Between the corporate IT network and the operational control network that runs physical processes","Between two conference room WiFi SSIDs","Inside the test lab only"],a:1,x:"Segmentation buys the most where compromise crosses a criticality boundary — corporate-to-OT is the highest-consequence crossing."},
{q:"SCENARIO: After a phishing exercise, 8% clicked and 1% reported. Six months of culture work later, 6% click and 34% report within an hour. The correct reading is:",o:["Failure — clicks should be zero","Major success — the reporting behavior that shortens dwell time improved 30-fold, which matters more than the click rate","No change worth noting","Proof that training should be punitive"],a:1,x:"Click rates plateau; the defensive win is fast reporting, which converts inevitable clicks into rapid containment."},
{q:"SCENARIO: A contractor must satisfy NIST 800-171, ISO 27001, and board CSF reporting. The efficient architecture is:",o:["Three parallel programs with separate evidence","One owned control inventory, one evidence repository, mapped outward to all three frameworks","Ignore two of the three","Rotate frameworks annually"],a:1,x:"Implement once, evidence once, map many — parallel programs triple cost and guarantee inter-copy drift."},
{q:"SCENARIO: An AO is asked to sign an ATO for a system whose SSP describes a boundary missing a two-year-old cloud interconnection. Signing as-is makes the authorization:",o:["Stronger, since less is documented","A risk decision made on materially false information — the package must be corrected first","Valid because the SCA missed it too","Permanent"],a:1,x:"The ATO is only as sound as the package's accuracy; a known-false boundary description invalidates the basis of the decision."},
{q:"SCENARIO: Budget allows either a tenth preventive tool or first-ever restoration testing of backups. The resilience-driven choice and reason:",o:["The tool — prevention always outranks recovery","Restoration testing — untested backups are hope, and recovery capability bounds worst-case mission damage","Neither, save the money","The tool, because testing is free"],a:1,x:"Marginal prevention shifts likelihood slightly; proven recovery caps consequence for every scenario including the ones prevention misses."},
{q:"SCENARIO: A dispatch system must lose no more than 15 minutes of data and be back in 4 hours. Which engineering pairing matches the requirement?",o:["Weekly full backups on tape stored onsite","5-minute database replication to a second site plus rehearsed failover timed under 4 hours, with immutable weekly snapshots","RAID 5 on the primary server","A second monitor for the administrator"],a:1,x:"RPO 15 minutes demands near-continuous replication; RTO 4 hours demands rehearsed, timed failover; ransomware demands an immutable copy independent of production credentials."},
{q:"SCENARIO: Two alerts fire the same credential-theft malware at a retailer and at a missile-component designer. Applying actor-motivation analysis, the designer's team should weight its response toward:",o:["Payment-system isolation, as with the retailer","Hunting lateral movement toward engineering data and persistence mechanisms, hypothesizing an intelligence-driven actor","Ignoring it, since malware is commodity","Public disclosure within the hour"],a:1,x:"The same commodity foothold serves different ends; a defense-industrial target must weight the APT hypothesis and hunt accordingly."}
]};
